</div><div mt-section-origin="Bookshelves/Analytical_Chemistry/Instrumental_Analysis_(LibreTexts)/35:_Appendicies/35.01:_Evaluation_of_Analytical_Data" class="mt-section" id="section_16"><span id="Robust_vs._Non-Robust_Measures_of_The_Center_and_Variation_About_the_Center"></span><h3 class="lt-chem-363066 editable">Robust vs. Non-Robust Measures of The Center and Variation About the Center</h3>

<p class="lt-chem-363066">A good question to ask is why we might desire more than one way to report the center of our data and the variation in our data about the center. Suppose that the result for the last of our 30 samples was reported as 483.17 instead of 48.317. Whether this is an accidental shifting of the decimal point or a true result is not relevant to us here; what matters is its effect on what we report. Here is a summary of the effect of this one value on each of our ways of summarizing our data.</p>

