</div><div mt-section-origin="Bookshelves/Analytical_Chemistry/Instrumental_Analysis_(LibreTexts)/35:_Appendicies/35.01:_Evaluation_of_Analytical_Data" class="mt-section" id="section_44"><span id="Grubb's_Test"></span><h4 class="lt-chem-363066 editable">Grubb's Test</h4>

<p class="lt-chem-363066">Although Dixon&rsquo;s <em>Q</em>-test is a common method for evaluating outliers, it is no longer favored by the International Standards Organization (ISO), which recommends the Grubb&rsquo;s<em><strong> </strong></em>test. There are several versions of Grubb&rsquo;s test depending on the number of potential outliers. Here we will consider the case where there is a single suspected outlier.</p>

<section class="box-note">
<div mt-section-origin="Bookshelves/Analytical_Chemistry/Instrumental_Analysis_(LibreTexts)/35:_Appendicies/35.01:_Evaluation_of_Analytical_Data" class="mt-section"><span id="Note_7"></span><h5 class="box-legend   lt-chem-363066 editable"><span class="lt-icon-default">Note</span></h5>

<p class="lt-chem-363066">For details on this recommendation, see International Standards ISO Guide 5752-2 &ldquo;Accuracy (trueness and precision) of measurement methods and results&ndash;Part 2: basic methods for the determination of repeatability and reproducibility of a standard measurement method,&rdquo; 1994.</p>
</div></section>

<p class="lt-chem-363066">The test statistic for Grubb&rsquo;s test, <em>G</em><sub>exp</sub>, is the distance between the sample&rsquo;s mean, \(\overline{X}\), and the potential outlier, \(X_\text{out}\), in terms of the sample&rsquo;s standard deviation, <em>s</em>.</p>

<p class="lt-chem-363066">\[G_\text{exp} = \frac {|X_\text{out} - \overline{X}|} {s} \nonumber \]</p>

<p class="lt-chem-363066">We compare the value of <em>G</em><sub>exp</sub> to a critical value \(G(\alpha, n)\), where \(\alpha\) is the probability that we will reject a valid data point and <em>n </em>is the number of data points in the sample. If <em>G</em><sub>exp</sub> is greater than \(G(\alpha, n)\), then we may reject the data point as an outlier, otherwise we retain the data point as part of the sample. Table \(\PageIndex{13}\) provides values for <em>G</em>(0.05, <em>n</em>) for a sample containing 3&ndash;10 values. A more extensive table is in Appendix 6. Values for \(G(\alpha, n)\) assume an underlying normal distribution.</p>

