</table>

<p class="lt-chem-363066">Adding the values in each column gives</p>

<p class="lt-chem-363066">\[\sum_{i = 1}^{n} x_i = 1.500 \quad \sum_{i = 1}^{n} y_i = 182.31 \quad \sum_{i = 1}^{n} x_i y_i = 66.701 \quad \sum_{i = 1}^{n} x_i^2 = 0.550 \nonumber \]</p>

<p class="lt-chem-363066">Substituting these values into the equations for the slope and the <em>y</em>-intercept gives</p>

<p class="lt-chem-363066">\[b_1 = \frac {(6 \times 66.701) - (1.500 \times 182.31)} {(6 \times 0.550) - (1.500)^2} = 120.706 \approx 120.71 \nonumber \]</p>

<p class="lt-chem-363066">\[b_0 = \frac {182.31 - (120.706 \times 1.500)} {6} = 0.209 \approx 0.21 \nonumber \]</p>

<p class="lt-chem-363066">The relationship between the signal, \(S\), and the analyte's concentration, \(C_A\), therefore, is</p>

<p class="lt-chem-363066">\[S = 120.71 \times C_A + 0.21 \nonumber \]</p>

<p class="lt-chem-363066">For now we keep two decimal places to match the number of decimal places in the signal. The resulting calibration curve is shown in Figure \(\PageIndex{28}\).</p>
